WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.661
`Drop` is Rust's structured cleanup
mechanism.

2
00:00:02.741 --> 00:00:04.849
When an owned value reaches the end of its
scope,

3
00:00:04.929 --> 00:00:06.826
Rust runs the cleanup logic for that
value.

4
00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:10.279
This deterministic behavior helps
developers reason about memory, files,

5
00:00:10.359 --> 00:00:11.940
sockets, buffers, locks, handles,

6
00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:15.709
and other resources without scattering
manual release calls throughout ordinary

7
00:00:15.789 --> 00:00:16.052
code.

8
00:00:16.132 --> 00:00:18.662
The important point is that cleanup is
tied to ownership.

9
00:00:18.742 --> 00:00:20.428
A value is dropped when its owner ends,

10
00:00:20.508 --> 00:00:22.511
not when any random reference stops using
it.

11
00:00:22.591 --> 00:00:26.016
That connection is what prevents safe
borrowed access from becoming cleanup

12
00:00:26.096 --> 00:00:29.521
authority and what keeps release behavior
aligned with the ownership model.

13
00:00:29.601 --> 00:00:33.238
Custom `Drop` implementations can be
useful when a type owns a resource that needs

14
00:00:33.318 --> 00:00:34.161
explicit release.

15
00:00:34.241 --> 00:00:36.823
They can close a file descriptor, release
a native handle,

16
00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:40.434
return a buffer to a pool, or notify
another component that ownership has ended.

17
00:00:40.514 --> 00:00:43.571
Because they define cleanup behavior, they
deserve careful review.

18
00:00:43.651 --> 00:00:45.073
Cleanup ordering also matters.

19
00:00:45.153 --> 00:00:47.261
Fields are dropped when their owner is
dropped.

20
00:00:47.341 --> 00:00:49.344
Partially initialized states, error paths,

21
00:00:49.424 --> 00:00:52.533
and wrapper types should be designed so
cleanup remains predictable.

22
00:00:52.613 --> 00:00:56.197
A resource-owning type should not rely on
scattered flags or external calls when

23
00:00:56.277 --> 00:00:58.543
ownership can express the lifecycle more
clearly.

24
00:00:58.623 --> 00:01:01.838
Defensive Rust keeps cleanup paths simple,
testable, and easy to audit.

25
00:01:01.918 --> 00:01:04.869
If the same resource appears to be
released through several paths,

26
00:01:04.949 --> 00:01:06.582
ask whether there is truly one owner.

27
00:01:06.662 --> 00:01:08.191
If manual release is still needed,

28
00:01:08.271 --> 00:01:11.380
document how it interacts with `Drop` so
cleanup cannot happen twice.
